In article <3s8fqm$6b7@gondor.sdsu.edu>, jernigan@mail.sdsu.edu (Wayne J. Jernigan) writes:
=How do I alter the User Buffer size for manipulating files?  I've been
=through the manuals and can't seem to figure it out!  Here are the
=particulars:
=
=Command Line:
=
=      diff  idwire.xls [users.todd.cdr.ed02.id]idwire.xls
=
=Error Return:
=
=      %DIFF-F-READERR, error reading IDWIRE.XLS;1
=      -RMS-W-RTB, 549 byte record too large for user's buffer
=
=Again, how (and with what utility) do I alter the user buffer in order
=to allow for a file of this size to be "compared"?

You don't.  You'd have to patch DIFF.EXE.  On the other hand, I suspect that
what's causing that error is that the longest record length field in
IDWIRE.XLS;1's file header is set improperly;  i.e., that DIFF is allocating a
user buffer based on the LRL field, and eventually reading a record that's
longer than that buffer.  To check this hypothesis, do a DIR/FULL on
IDWIR.XLS;1 and see what it says the longest record in the file is.  If it's
something lss than 549, then try using CONVERT to to make a copy of the file,
thn use DIFF on that.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Carl J Lydick | INTERnet: CARL@SOL1.GPS.CALTECH.EDU | NSI/HEPnet: SOL1::CARL

